%YAML 1.1
%TAG !u! tag:unity3d.com,2011:
--- !u!114 &11400000
MonoBehaviour:
  m_ObjectHideFlags: 0
  m_CorrespondingSourceObject: {fileID: 0}
  m_PrefabInstance: {fileID: 0}
  m_PrefabAsset: {fileID: 0}
  m_GameObject: {fileID: 0}
  m_Enabled: 1
  m_EditorHideFlags: 0
  m_Script: {fileID: 11500000, guid: e698ea0e692a4af0918d779463adb1c9, type: 3}
  m_Name: Story
  m_EditorClassIdentifier: 
  customers:
  - name: Young Postman
    emotion: Anxiety to Calm
    dialogue:
    - Day 1. A young postman arrives before his morning round. His fingers keep folding
      the edge of a delivery slip.
    - 'Postman: Is this the tuning shop? I heard you help when a tune will not settle.'
    - 'Tuner: I have only just taken over. But you can sit down. What keeps returning
      to you?'
    - 'Postman: My first week, I delivered a letter to the wrong address. I got it
      back, but I cannot stop thinking about it.'
    - 'Postman: Now I read every address again and again. Even after I know it is
      right, I go back to check.'
    - 'Tuner: Does checking help you feel ready to leave?'
    - 'Postman: For a second. Then I wonder whether I missed something. My whole
      round falls behind.'
    - 'Tuner: We do not have to take away your care. Perhaps we can give it a steadier
      rhythm.'
    - 'Postman: A rhythm I can trust? I would like to hear that.'
    - 'Tuner: Rest your hand on the lid. Let the music box listen.'
    - The box answers with a hurried melody and uneven mechanical sounds.
    reply: 'Postman: The tune is still quick. But it does not feel as though it is
      chasing me anymore.


      Tuner: It is still your melody. There is simply
      room to breathe between the notes.


      Postman: I will check the address.
      Once, carefully. Then I will try to keep walking.


      Tuner: That sounds
      like a place to start.


      The postman folds the delivery slip and puts
      it away.'
    structure: 0
    tone: 4
    drift: 0.07
    wave: 0.22
    frequency: 0.9
    impulse: 0
    mostlyHold: 0
  - name: Street Guitarist
    emotion: Anger to Understanding
    dialogue:
    - Day 4. A guitarist arrives with a worn instrument case. He taps a sharp rhythm
      against its handle.
    - 'Guitarist: My partner says I never leave him any room. I say he never plays
      what we agreed.'
    - 'Tuner: You have been playing together for a long time?'
    - 'Guitarist: Since we used to set up outside the bookshop. We could draw a crowd
      with almost nothing.'
    - 'Guitarist: Now he wants to improvise every set. I work on the arrangement,
      and he changes it in front of everyone.'
    - 'Tuner: What do you hear when he changes it?'
    - 'Guitarist: That what I brought was not enough. So I play harder, and he plays
      over me. Last time we stopped speaking.'
    - 'Tuner: Could his reply be an invitation, even if the way he offers it hurts?'
    - 'Guitarist: Maybe. I am not ready to say he was right.'
    - 'Tuner: You do not have to. Let us first hear both parts without making either
      disappear.'
    - He rests his hand on the lid. Strong beats collide with rough, displaced accents.
    reply: 'Guitarist: It still has some bite. Good. I did not want you to make it
      polite.


      Tuner: The strength can stay. Listen to the space where the
      other phrase answers.


      Guitarist: I can hear it now. He was trying to
      join in. Not every time, perhaps. But some of the time.


      Guitarist:
      I should listen to our old recordings before I decide there is nothing left
      to say.


      Tuner: You can decide what to send after that.'
    structure: 1
    tone: 5
    drift: 0.07
    wave: 0.22
    frequency: 0.9
    impulse: 0.8
    mostlyHold: 0
  - name: Bookshop Owner
    emotion: Loss to Acceptance
    dialogue:
    - 'Owner: Hello there. Hope I''m not interrupting anything.'
    - 'Tuner: Not at all. Please come in.'
    - 'Owner: Thanks. I run the bookshop around the corner. You might''ve seen it.'
    - 'Tuner: Oh, yeah! I walk past it every day.'
    - 'Owner: I''ve been meaning to stop by. Used to know the previous owner of this
      place.'
    - 'Owner: But I never thought I''d come here looking for help.'
    - 'Owner: Anyway... I''m closing the bookshop soon. After more than twenty years...'
    - 'Tuner: Oh... I''m sorry to hear that.'
    - 'Owner: I knew this day would come eventually.'
    - 'Owner: It''s just... You spend so long in one place, you start thinking it''ll
      always be there...'
    - 'Tuner: Twenty years is such a long time.'
    - 'Owner: It is. I''ve watched so many kids grow up. Some of them even come back
      with their babies now.'
    - 'Owner: And I keep thinking... once I lock that door for the last time, will
      any of it matter anymore?'
    - 'Tuner: I believe it means a lot to you.'
    - 'Owner: Yeah. More than I realised, I think... These days, the sadness keeps
      me awake most nights.'
    - 'Owner: May I try that magic music box? I''ve heard things about it.'
    - 'Tuner: Of course. Just put your hand on the lid.'
    - 'Owner: ...Huh. That reminds me of the shop after everyone''s gone home.'
    - 'Tuner: Let''s see what we can do.'
    reply: 'Owner: That''s lovely... Still a little sad, though.


      Tuner: I
      guess that''s alright. You don''t have to stop being sad about it.


      Owner:
      You know, I''ve been so busy thinking about the shop closing, I almost forgot
      how much happened while it was open.


      Owner: Oh! Actually, let me show
      you something.


      Owner: I''m planning a memory exhibition. These are
      some of the photos that''ll be on display.


      Tuner: Wow, meaningful !


      Owner:
      I''ve got years of photos, old notes, little things people left behind.


      Owner:
      I was just going to put them away, but... maybe I should show them to more
      people.


      Tuner: That sounds wonderful. I''d love to come.


      Owner:
      Really? Well, see you then!


      Owner: I''m still going to miss the place.
      But maybe closing the shop doesn''t mean losing everything that happened there.


      Tuner:
      I''m sure a lot of people will be happy to see those memories.


      Owner:
      I hope so. Well then, I''d better get things ready. Thank you, dear.'
    structure: 2
    tone: 4
    drift: 0.19
    wave: 0.1
    frequency: 0.3
    impulse: 0
    mostlyHold: 1
  - name: Old Photographer
    emotion: Longing to Hope
    dialogue:
    - Day 12. An older photographer enters. A camera hangs at his side; an unfinished
      envelope rests in his coat pocket.
    - 'Photographer: The bookshop owner told me about your music box. I am Michael.'
    - 'Tuner: I saw your signature in her album. The musicians, and the postman at
      the window.'
    - 'Michael: I gave her those pictures before she planned the display. A street
      changes quietly. I wanted her to have something of it.'
    - 'Tuner: You have kept many people''s memories.'
    - 'Michael: More easily than my own, it seems. A friend and I once meant to travel
      together and make an album.'
    - 'Michael: We kept postponing it. Then our letters became shorter, and then
      there were no letters at all.'
    - 'Tuner: Do you know where your friend lives?'
    - 'Michael: I still have the address. I have written it on this envelope more
      times than I care to admit.'
    - 'Michael: I do not know how to explain the years. The album is unfinished.
      I have nothing grand enough to send.'
    - 'Tuner: A letter does not have to finish the whole story. It can ask whether
      there is room for another page.'
    - 'Michael: Then let us hear what an unfinished song can do.'
    - He rests his hand on the lid. A solitary melody leaves room for an answer that
      has not yet arrived.
    reply: 'Michael: That second voice does not answer every note.


      Tuner:
      It can leave some space. The first voice is still there.


      Michael: I
      have been waiting to send the perfect letter, as though that could make up
      for all the quiet.


      Michael: I could simply say I remember. And ask
      how my friend has been.


      Tuner: You already know the address.


      Michael
      takes out the envelope. This time, he begins the letter.'
    structure: 3
    tone: 5
    drift: 0.07
    wave: 0.22
    frequency: 0.9
    impulse: 0.45
    mostlyHold: 0
  parts:
  - name: Steady Wheel
    structure: 1
    description: Steadies a rushed or drifting beat.
  - name: Sync Wheel
    structure: 1
    description: Brings clashing rhythms together.
  - name: Sustain Spring
    structure: 1
    description: Connects broken phrases and holds notes.
  - name: Echo Wheel
    structure: 1
    description: Adds a gentle reply to a lone melody.
  - name: Soft Plate
    structure: 0
    description: Softens sharp sounds.
  - name: Warm Plate
    structure: 0
    description: Adds warmth and depth.
